          "question": "Burning when I pee and going more often since yesterday, no temperature.",
          "answer": "**Urgency: routine, based on what you've described so far.** The urgency tool found no emergency pattern in what you wrote. That isn't a clearance, and it can't check things you haven't mentioned. Symptom details from the urinary tract infection reference (Urinary tract infection, General clinical reference) say that a lower urinary tract presentation typically includes burning on urination, more frequent urination, and sometimes cloudy or strong-smelling urine. The same reference says fever, flank or back pain, nausea or vomiting, and feeling generally unwell move the picture out of the routine category.\n\n**Seek urgent care now if any of these apply:**\n- Pain in your side or back, below the ribs (flank pain)\n- Blood in your urine\n- Vomiting, or being unable to keep fluids down\n- Confusion, unusual drowsiness, or feeling very unwell\n- Not passing urine, or severe dehydration\n- You are pregnant or think you might be\n\n**Tell a clinician soon if:** symptoms get worse, a fever starts, or they haven't improved within a day or two. The reference also notes that older adults can present differently, with confusion or falls rather than typical urinary symptoms.\n\n**Body systems:** The symptom analysis tool did not recognize this pattern and returned no body-system grouping. The reference above places these symptoms in the urinary system. Only a clinician can say what is causing them. Please don't treat the other conditions the tool listed as relevant to you, because they do not match your symptoms.\n\nIf you tell me whether any of the red flags above are present, I can reassess the urgency.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
